1085
00:57:28,979 --> 00:57:31,539
I can find my way out.

1086
00:57:33,217 --> 00:57:35,083
Tomorrow.

1087
00:57:36,453 --> 00:57:38,120
Yeah.

1088
00:58:29,506 --> 00:58:32,066
Hey.

1089
00:58:34,378 --> 00:58:35,521
Hi, Michelle. Sit down.

1090
00:58:35,545 --> 00:58:37,223
Hi. You didn't have any trouble?

1091
00:58:37,247 --> 00:58:38,579
No, no.

1092
00:58:38,648 --> 00:58:41,283
Hey, my little evening
with David paid off.

1093
00:58:41,351 --> 00:58:42,963
I have something. All right.

1094
00:58:42,987 --> 00:58:44,686
I got a look at his date book.

1095
00:58:44,754 --> 00:58:46,254
He travels a lot.

1096
00:58:46,323 --> 00:58:48,723
Europe, South
America, the Bahamas.

1097
00:58:48,792 --> 00:58:51,259
Oh. The finest hotels.

1098
00:58:51,328 --> 00:58:52,560
You wonder when he has time

1099
00:58:52,629 --> 00:58:54,429
to take care of his
real estate business.

1100
00:58:54,497 --> 00:58:57,344
So, I called the Illinois
Board of Realty. Oh?

1101
00:58:57,368 --> 00:58:58,578
He's not in real estate.

1102
00:58:58,602 --> 00:59:00,735
Yes, he is... in a big way.

1103
00:59:00,804 --> 00:59:02,404
He got into it overnight.

1104
00:59:02,472 --> 00:59:05,072
He bought a row of houses,
he tore them all down

1105
00:59:05,141 --> 00:59:07,108
and put up new, very
expensive houses.

1106
00:59:07,177 --> 00:59:08,288
Oh. They sell?

1107
00:59:08,312 --> 00:59:09,377
Not yet.

1108
00:59:10,481 --> 00:59:12,213
Takes a lot of money.

1109
00:59:12,282 --> 00:59:13,581
Yeah.

1110
00:59:13,651 --> 00:59:15,462
Sorry I'm late. MATLOCK:
Oh, sit down, Conrad.

1111
00:59:15,486 --> 00:59:16,785
Conrad McMasters,

1112
00:59:16,854 --> 00:59:17,931
Michelle Thomas, my associate.

1113
00:59:17,955 --> 00:59:19,199
How are you? Hi. Good.

1114
00:59:19,223 --> 00:59:21,623
Just talked to
the Boston Police.

1115
00:59:21,692 --> 00:59:24,859
You call them, they ask you
what department you want.

1116
00:59:24,928 --> 00:59:26,961
See, now, that's a police force.

1117
00:59:28,298 --> 00:59:29,964
Anyway, Ernie D'Amato...

1118
00:59:30,034 --> 00:59:31,433
He's got a restaurant in town.

1119
00:59:31,502 --> 00:59:32,800
Not doing too good.

1120
00:59:32,869 --> 00:59:34,503
No liquor license.

1121
00:59:34,571 --> 00:59:37,272
Now, the police record...
Busted six years ago.

1122
00:59:37,341 --> 00:59:39,741
Cocaine: possession
with intent to sell.

1123
00:59:39,810 --> 00:59:42,977
Well, we've all
made phone calls.

1124
00:59:43,046 --> 00:59:46,615
A friend in oil in Houston...
Knows everybody.

1125
00:59:46,684 --> 00:59:50,786
Uh, he said that Lee Kramer
is known for three things.

1126
00:59:50,854 --> 00:59:53,021
Of course, he, uh...
he likes women.

1127
00:59:53,090 --> 00:59:55,302
Puts pistachio nuts in his,

1128
00:59:55,326 --> 00:59:56,358
uh, chili.

1129
00:59:56,427 --> 00:59:59,327
Keeps drilling oil
wells that turn up dry.

1130
00:59:59,396 --> 01:00:01,407
But they're spending
money like they invented it.

1131
01:00:01,431 --> 01:00:02,542
Yeah.

1132
01:00:02,566 --> 01:00:06,568
Somebody put that
cocaine in my car.

1133
01:00:06,637 --> 01:00:09,070
They're running drugs.

1134
01:00:12,876 --> 01:00:14,153
The Lost Colony.

1135
01:00:14,177 --> 01:00:15,488
McMASTERS: Yeah.

1136
01:00:15,512 --> 01:00:17,045
It's a good, good place

1137
01:00:17,114 --> 01:00:19,014
It's been going on every summer

1138
01:00:19,082 --> 01:00:20,281
for 50-some years.

1139
01:00:20,350 --> 01:00:22,350
Mm. This is where the...

1140
01:00:22,419 --> 01:00:24,197
the first English colony lived?

1141
01:00:24,221 --> 01:00:25,661
Mm-hmm, on Roanoke Island.

1142
01:00:25,722 --> 01:00:27,889
And no one knows
what happened to them?

1143
01:00:27,957 --> 01:00:30,091
Mm-mm, no.

1144
01:00:30,160 --> 01:00:33,128
You know, the
old-timers say that...

1145
01:00:33,196 --> 01:00:36,498
on a full moon, a pure white doe

1146
01:00:36,566 --> 01:00:39,667
can be seen out
on the sand dunes.

1147
01:00:39,736 --> 01:00:43,405
They say that's the
spirit of Virginia Dare.

1148
01:00:43,473 --> 01:00:45,206
Uh-huh?

1149
01:00:45,275 --> 01:00:47,175
Gives you the chills, don't it?

1150
01:00:47,244 --> 01:00:48,687
Yeah. Yeah.

1151
01:00:48,711 --> 01:00:49,711
Ah.

1152
01:00:49,780 --> 01:00:50,780
What's going on?

1153
01:00:50,847 --> 01:00:52,993
What's this all about? Well,

1154
01:00:53,017 --> 01:00:55,328
uh, we're-we're making
progress with your case,

1155
01:00:55,352 --> 01:00:59,354
but we have something to tell
you that isn't, uh, simple or pleasant.

1156
01:00:59,423 --> 01:01:01,289
Let's have it.

1157
01:01:01,358 --> 01:01:04,492
We believe your brother and
Ryan Bartell and the others

1158
01:01:04,561 --> 01:01:05,894
were running drugs.

1159
01:01:07,831 --> 01:01:10,264
The remaining three still are.

1160
01:01:10,334 --> 01:01:12,801
That's what Frank
meant in his last letter.

1161
01:01:12,869 --> 01:01:14,836
He said he was
involved in something

1162
01:01:14,904 --> 01:01:17,005
that had to go
sour sooner or later.

1163
01:01:17,074 --> 01:01:19,541
That's, uh...

1164
01:01:19,609 --> 01:01:21,655
that's why he was killed.

1165
01:01:21,679 --> 01:01:23,923
McMASTERS: He wanted out.

1166
01:01:23,947 --> 01:01:26,448
David arranged a meeting
with someone for 2:00 today

1167
01:01:26,516 --> 01:01:27,782
at Bags Bay.

1168
01:01:27,851 --> 01:01:29,050
I didn't hear everything,

1169
01:01:29,118 --> 01:01:32,654
but I did hear him say,
"Make it a twin if you can."

1170
01:01:32,722 --> 01:01:35,323
Twin-engine seaplane?

1171
01:01:36,560 --> 01:01:39,494
Running drugs
from South America.

1172
01:01:39,563 --> 01:01:41,530
I think he could've
arranged something.

1173
01:01:41,598 --> 01:01:44,544
McMASTERS: A party? - Yeah.

1174
01:01:44,568 --> 01:01:46,501
Get the sheriff
and make it official?

1175
01:01:46,570 --> 01:01:48,336
Yeah.

1176
01:01:56,079 --> 01:01:58,580
All right, you boys,
spread out that way.

1177
01:01:58,649 --> 01:02:01,282
Jackson, head over that way.

1178
01:02:01,351 --> 01:02:02,751
Come on.

1179
01:02:02,819 --> 01:02:05,379
Down over here
on this side, go on.

1180
01:02:09,393 --> 01:02:11,993
All right, now, let's
see what you got.

1181
01:02:14,264 --> 01:02:16,364
It's almost 2:00.

1182
01:02:16,433 --> 01:02:18,366
Where the hell are they?

1183
01:02:18,435 --> 01:02:20,202
If the plane
shows, they'll show.

1184
01:02:20,270 --> 01:02:22,830
There's something!

1185
01:02:24,341 --> 01:02:25,707
That's it.

1186
01:02:35,285 --> 01:02:38,119
Clarence, everybody
keep down and sit tight.

1187
01:02:59,777 --> 01:03:02,410
Something's wrong.

1188
01:03:07,518 --> 01:03:08,650
He's pulling out.

1189
01:03:08,719 --> 01:03:10,196
Clarence, hit the beach. Now.

1190
01:03:10,220 --> 01:03:12,220
You in the plane,
hold it right there!

1191
01:03:12,288 --> 01:03:14,255
Out of the plane!
Hold it right there!

1192
01:03:14,324 --> 01:03:15,590
Don't move!

1193
01:03:15,659 --> 01:03:18,219
Come on, get out of there!

1194
01:03:19,530 --> 01:03:22,163
All right, easy, easy.

1195
01:03:22,232 --> 01:03:24,132
I don't get it. Why
didn't they show?

1196
01:03:24,201 --> 01:03:25,133
Conrad!

1197
01:03:25,202 --> 01:03:26,880
Well, at least we got the drugs.

1198
01:03:26,904 --> 01:03:28,936
Check if they're in the back.

1199
01:03:29,006 --> 01:03:31,673
We don't have
diddly! It's empty!

1200
01:03:31,742 --> 01:03:32,952
Nothing. What?!

1201
01:03:32,976 --> 01:03:34,454
Conrad... Rip
everything out of there.

1202
01:03:34,478 --> 01:03:36,945
Rip out the seat cushions,
pontoons... Move! Come on!

1203
01:03:37,013 --> 01:03:38,412
There's nothing
in there, Conrad!

1204
01:03:38,482 --> 01:03:39,959
There's got to be
something in there!

1205
01:03:39,983 --> 01:03:42,183
Do you have any
idea how much it costs

1206
01:03:42,252 --> 01:03:44,496
to bring these men and
equipment out here, Conrad?

1207
01:03:44,520 --> 01:03:46,499
I'll see you back at the office.

1208
01:03:46,523 --> 01:03:50,758
Man. He... he's
gonna have my head.

1209
01:03:59,069 --> 01:04:01,302
Hey, fella.

1210
01:04:01,371 --> 01:04:03,805
I want to talk to you.

1211
01:04:06,343 --> 01:04:08,943
<i>What the hell happened?</i>

1212
01:04:09,012 --> 01:04:11,445
Calm down, all they got
was an empty airplane.

1213
01:04:11,515 --> 01:04:12,692
We needed that plane.

1214
01:04:12,716 --> 01:04:15,817
All right.

1215
01:04:15,886 --> 01:04:17,651
We'll use the backup.

1216
01:04:17,721 --> 01:04:18,964
They're still over
at the sheriff's office

1217
01:04:18,988 --> 01:04:21,256
trying to figure it out.

1218
01:04:22,492 --> 01:04:24,425
The boat.

1219
01:04:24,494 --> 01:04:25,960
Fast.

1220
01:04:26,029 --> 01:04:28,589
How did they know
about the plane?

1221
01:04:30,200 --> 01:04:32,133
One of us spilled it.

1222
01:04:36,273 --> 01:04:39,040
You were in a dinner
last night, David.

1223
01:04:39,109 --> 01:04:42,043
I told you I had a date.

1224
01:04:43,113 --> 01:04:46,381
This blonde... who is she?

1225
01:04:46,450 --> 01:04:48,583
Just a blonde.

1226
01:04:49,653 --> 01:04:51,753
She's just a...

1227
01:04:55,892 --> 01:04:57,871
I think she was
looking at my date book.

1228
01:04:57,895 --> 01:05:00,561
You suppose she is
working with that lawyer?

1229
01:05:00,630 --> 01:05:02,731
You jackass.

1230
01:05:07,570 --> 01:05:11,672
Be on the dock across from
the<i> Elizabeth II,</i> 5:00 sharp.

1231
01:05:58,255 --> 01:06:00,188
Any sign of David?

1232
01:06:57,013 --> 01:06:58,190
Come on.

1233
01:06:58,214 --> 01:06:59,925
All right? Yeah.

1234
01:07:01,618 --> 01:07:06,054
Home free, boys,
we're home free.

1235
01:07:12,629 --> 01:07:14,640
Pull it alongside.

1236
01:07:14,664 --> 01:07:16,831
Just toss these right in.

1237
01:07:20,270 --> 01:07:22,103
Everybody, freeze!

1238
01:07:22,172 --> 01:07:25,092
All right, all of you,
hit the deck, come on.

1239
01:07:26,776 --> 01:07:28,754
Hands in plain
sight, move, come on!

1240
01:07:28,778 --> 01:07:30,044
Come on, let's go.

1241
01:07:31,915 --> 01:07:33,180
Okay, clear.

1242
01:07:36,920 --> 01:07:39,854
Well, I got to hand it to
you, Conrad, you were right.

1243
01:07:39,923 --> 01:07:42,056
Yeah, well, that seaplane pilot

1244
01:07:42,125 --> 01:07:43,869
this afternoon
realized, you know,

1245
01:07:43,893 --> 01:07:45,304
looking at the pros and cons,

1246
01:07:45,328 --> 01:07:47,161
that if he cooperated with me,

1247
01:07:47,230 --> 01:07:48,629
he might limit his time in jail.

1248
01:07:48,698 --> 01:07:49,630
Yeah, so...

1249
01:07:49,699 --> 01:07:51,565
So he told me about the backup.

1250
01:07:51,634 --> 01:07:54,368
I wonder if the sheriff'll
give me my head back now.

1251
01:07:54,437 --> 01:07:56,182
Oh, yeah, he'll give it back

1252
01:07:56,206 --> 01:07:59,173
and make sure it looks nice.

1253
01:08:00,243 --> 01:08:01,942
What's the charge?

1254
01:08:02,012 --> 01:08:04,846
Conspiracy to deliver a
controlled substance, maybe?

1255
01:08:05,949 --> 01:08:07,949
Try tax evasion.

1256
01:08:08,018 --> 01:08:10,418
They weren't smuggling drugs in,

1257
01:08:10,487 --> 01:08:12,920
they were smuggling
drug money out.

1258
01:08:31,341 --> 01:08:33,319
Hey, hey, hey, wait a
minute, wait a minute.

1259
01:08:33,343 --> 01:08:35,643
What?

1260
01:08:35,711 --> 01:08:38,346
When you arrested
these three birds,

1261
01:08:38,414 --> 01:08:41,983
they were each carrying
a million-and-a-half dollars.

1262
01:08:42,052 --> 01:08:43,963
That's right. And according
to the seaplane pilot,

1263
01:08:43,987 --> 01:08:47,956
they were illegally
moving it to offshore banks.

1264
01:08:48,024 --> 01:08:48,957
Yeah.

1265
01:08:49,025 --> 01:08:50,158
No cocaine?

1266
01:08:50,227 --> 01:08:51,325
That's right.

1267
01:08:51,394 --> 01:08:53,327
We searched the houses

1268
01:08:53,396 --> 01:08:54,528
and no cocaine.

1269
01:08:54,597 --> 01:08:55,529
That's right,

1270
01:08:55,598 --> 01:08:56,609
they don't deal drugs here,

1271
01:08:56,633 --> 01:08:58,732
they just bring
the money through.

1272
01:08:58,802 --> 01:09:01,735
Where did the cocaine
found in my car come from?

1273
01:09:01,805 --> 01:09:02,805
Uh...

1274
01:09:02,839 --> 01:09:05,017
Suppose there's somebody else?

1275
01:09:05,041 --> 01:09:06,652
Somebody else? Yeah.

1276
01:09:06,676 --> 01:09:11,312
They were taking their
money out of Manteo.

1277
01:09:11,381 --> 01:09:13,414
Where is Ryan Bartell's money?

1278
01:09:18,454 --> 01:09:21,189
Maybe the other
three divided it up.

1279
01:09:21,257 --> 01:09:23,991
Or there's another two
suitcases hidden somewhere.

1280
01:09:24,060 --> 01:09:26,860
Maybe there's some
connection with that

1281
01:09:26,929 --> 01:09:28,762
and the drugs found in your car.

1282
01:09:28,831 --> 01:09:31,391
That's what I'm saying.

1283
01:09:35,238 --> 01:09:37,798
Oh, yeah, they got bail.

1284
01:09:40,577 --> 01:09:42,977
<i>I understand you do</i>

1285
01:09:43,046 --> 01:09:45,647
a pretty good business
at your garage, Mr. Rose.

1286
01:09:45,715 --> 01:09:47,159
I got the best garage in town.

1287
01:09:47,183 --> 01:09:48,115
Of course,

1288
01:09:48,184 --> 01:09:50,084
you don't really own it, do you?

1289
01:09:50,152 --> 01:09:51,452
It's owned by the bank

1290
01:09:51,521 --> 01:09:53,588
over in Little Washington,
North Carolina,

1291
01:09:53,656 --> 01:09:55,523
or that is, it's
owned by the men

1292
01:09:55,592 --> 01:09:58,425
who hold the note that
the bank collects on.

1293
01:09:58,495 --> 01:10:00,561
Is that right?

1294
01:10:00,630 --> 01:10:01,763
Well, yeah.

1295
01:10:01,831 --> 01:10:03,998
Whose names are on the note?

1296
01:10:04,067 --> 01:10:06,634
I don't remember.

1297
01:10:08,738 --> 01:10:12,239
The names on the
note are Lee Kramer,

1298
01:10:12,309 --> 01:10:14,809
David Channing, Ernie D'Amato,

1299
01:10:14,878 --> 01:10:17,478
and the late Frank
Hamilton and Ryan Bartell.

1300
01:10:17,546 --> 01:10:18,713
Is that right?

1301
01:10:18,782 --> 01:10:21,182
I guess so.

1302
01:10:21,251 --> 01:10:24,151
And you've missed payments
now and then, haven't you?

1303
01:10:24,221 --> 01:10:25,231
Well, m-my divorce caused me...

1304
01:10:25,255 --> 01:10:28,256
And they didn't foreclose?

1305
01:10:28,325 --> 01:10:29,456
No.

1306
01:10:29,526 --> 01:10:34,195
Do you, uh, do you
do things for them?

1307
01:10:34,263 --> 01:10:37,131
I-I fix their cars.

1308
01:10:37,200 --> 01:10:40,034
Do you lie for them?

1309
01:10:40,103 --> 01:10:41,435
Objection.

1310
01:10:41,504 --> 01:10:43,004
Sustained.

1311
01:10:43,073 --> 01:10:46,040
Didn't my client call you
the evening of the ninth

1312
01:10:46,109 --> 01:10:49,677
and ask you to come by
his house the next morning

1313
01:10:49,746 --> 01:10:50,945
and fix his car?

1314
01:10:51,014 --> 01:10:53,480
I wasn't anywhere near
his house that morning.

1315
01:10:55,585 --> 01:10:57,685
Uh, Mr. Rose,

1316
01:10:57,754 --> 01:11:02,557
do you own a 1962

1317
01:11:02,626 --> 01:11:04,759
Porsche Cabriolet?

1318
01:11:04,827 --> 01:11:08,629
Yeah. Restored it myself.

1319
01:11:08,698 --> 01:11:11,666
D-Do you drive it or do you
keep it under a roof somewhere?

1320
01:11:11,735 --> 01:11:12,967
No, I drive it.

1321
01:11:13,036 --> 01:11:15,469
I mean, I, you know, put
a cover on it when I park it,

1322
01:11:15,538 --> 01:11:16,838
keep it in the garage at night.

1323
01:11:16,906 --> 01:11:17,906
Yeah.

1324
01:11:17,974 --> 01:11:20,152
Does anybody else
on the island have

1325
01:11:20,176 --> 01:11:21,176
a car like that?

1326
01:11:22,178 --> 01:11:23,110
No, sir.

1327
01:11:23,179 --> 01:11:27,281
So it was your Porsche Cabriolet

1328
01:11:27,349 --> 01:11:28,749
that went through

1329
01:11:28,818 --> 01:11:32,219
Roy's Car Wash at 8:30
the morning of the murder.

1330
01:11:32,289 --> 01:11:34,755
How do you know that?

1331
01:11:34,824 --> 01:11:37,591
Roy Scarborough told me.

1332
01:11:37,660 --> 01:11:41,895
He owns the car wash, says
you came in with fresh tree sap

1333
01:11:41,965 --> 01:11:43,642
on the hood of your car
and were real anxious

1334
01:11:43,666 --> 01:11:44,999
to get it off
before it soaked in

1335
01:11:45,068 --> 01:11:46,000
and ruined the paint.

1336
01:11:46,069 --> 01:11:47,069
Now, how...

1337
01:11:48,371 --> 01:11:52,239
could a car that was
in a garage all night

1338
01:11:52,308 --> 01:11:56,644
wind up with tree sap on
it at 8:30 in the morning?

1339
01:11:57,747 --> 01:11:59,947
I don't know.

1340
01:12:00,016 --> 01:12:02,316
That's a tough one, isn't it?

1341
01:12:04,387 --> 01:12:10,624
I think that sap came
from that big tree

1342
01:12:10,693 --> 01:12:13,327
outside Spencer
Hamilton's house.

1343
01:12:13,396 --> 01:12:18,433
Roy told me that he
was all the time scraping

1344
01:12:18,501 --> 01:12:20,434
that same kind of sap

1345
01:12:20,503 --> 01:12:22,336
off Frank's car.

1346
01:12:24,741 --> 01:12:27,374
You were there the morning
of the murder, weren't you?

1347
01:12:27,444 --> 01:12:30,010
All right, I-I-I was there.

1348
01:12:30,080 --> 01:12:32,446
And my client was
there, too, wasn't he?

1349
01:12:32,515 --> 01:12:34,181
Yeah, he was there.

1350
01:12:34,250 --> 01:12:36,150
Who told you to lie?

1351
01:12:39,188 --> 01:12:42,022
The, the... man
who hold my note.

1352
01:12:42,092 --> 01:12:44,392
There's three of them
left. Which one was it?

1353
01:12:44,461 --> 01:12:45,738
I don't, I don't know.

1354
01:12:45,762 --> 01:12:49,196
I didn't, uh... I didn't
recognize the voice.

1355
01:12:49,265 --> 01:12:52,499
See, one of them, uh, called me,

1356
01:12:52,568 --> 01:12:55,236
and he threatened
to take my garage,

1357
01:12:55,304 --> 01:12:57,871
and he told me what
to do, and I did it.

1358
01:13:00,142 --> 01:13:01,809
No further questions.

1359
01:13:01,878 --> 01:13:05,045
Cross-examine.

1360
01:13:05,114 --> 01:13:08,349
Mr. Rose, was the
defendant standing right there

1361
01:13:08,417 --> 01:13:10,083
while you were fixing his car?

1362
01:13:10,153 --> 01:13:12,453
Well, no. He...

1363
01:13:12,522 --> 01:13:13,522
Where was he?

1364
01:13:13,590 --> 01:13:16,090
He went in the house.

1365
01:13:16,159 --> 01:13:17,439
And from where you were working,

1366
01:13:17,493 --> 01:13:19,138
could you see to the
back door of the house?

1367
01:13:19,162 --> 01:13:21,795
No.

1368
01:13:21,865 --> 01:13:23,876
So it's entirely possible
that at one point,

1369
01:13:23,900 --> 01:13:26,133
he left the house
through the back door,

1370
01:13:26,202 --> 01:13:29,770
met Ryan Bartell in the
woods somewhere, shot him,

1371
01:13:29,839 --> 01:13:32,640
and returned to the house
without you seeing him,

1372
01:13:32,709 --> 01:13:34,608
isn't it, Mr. Rose?

1373
01:13:36,913 --> 01:13:38,179
Yeah.

1374
01:13:38,248 --> 01:13:40,614
Sure is possible.

1375
01:13:40,683 --> 01:13:42,516
No further questions.

1376
01:13:49,258 --> 01:13:50,757
Hey, Gerry, how's it going?

1377
01:13:50,826 --> 01:13:52,237
It's going, Conrad. What's up?

1378
01:13:52,261 --> 01:13:54,507
You remember that cocaine
bust out of Nags Head last month?

1379
01:13:54,531 --> 01:13:55,863
Last month...?

1380
01:13:55,932 --> 01:13:57,130
Rudy Clark,

1381
01:13:57,200 --> 01:13:59,400
12 ounces. Mind if I have
a look at the evidence?

1382
01:14:02,972 --> 01:14:05,740
Anything for Manteo's finest.

1383
01:14:05,808 --> 01:14:06,808
Got the inventory?

1384
01:14:06,876 --> 01:14:09,436
It's there with the evidence.

1385
01:14:16,318 --> 01:14:19,286
Eight?

1386
01:14:19,355 --> 01:14:21,688
Well, Commissioner,
even though you say

1387
01:14:21,757 --> 01:14:22,990
you're not co-counsel.

1388
01:14:23,059 --> 01:14:26,594
The fact is the state of
North Carolina requires

1389
01:14:26,663 --> 01:14:31,131
a local co-counsel when
counsel is from another state.

1390
01:14:31,200 --> 01:14:33,934
And for Mr. Hamilton,
you're it, right?

1391
01:14:34,003 --> 01:14:36,603
Yes, sir, that is correct.

1392
01:14:36,673 --> 01:14:39,006
You don't mind being
called as a witness, do you?

1393
01:14:39,075 --> 01:14:41,308
Oh, no, no, not at all.

1394
01:14:41,377 --> 01:14:43,845
I just don't know how
much help I can be.

1395
01:14:43,913 --> 01:14:45,579
Maybe more than you think.

1396
01:14:45,648 --> 01:14:48,516
You practice law in
Manteo, don't you?

1397
01:14:48,584 --> 01:14:49,828
I have a little practice.

1398
01:14:49,852 --> 01:14:54,288
Yeah. In fact, you're
the attorney of record

1399
01:14:54,356 --> 01:15:00,695
in a cocaine possession
case against Mr. Rudy Clark,

1400
01:15:00,763 --> 01:15:01,695
is that right?

1401
01:15:01,764 --> 01:15:02,807
Well...

1402
01:15:02,831 --> 01:15:03,997
Yes or no?

1403
01:15:04,066 --> 01:15:05,066
Yes.

1404
01:15:05,101 --> 01:15:08,435
How much cocaine was seized

1405
01:15:08,504 --> 01:15:10,115
at the time of
Mr. Clark's arrest?

1406
01:15:10,139 --> 01:15:11,072
Well, it was...

1407
01:15:11,140 --> 01:15:12,539
12 ounces, wasn't it?

1408
01:15:12,608 --> 01:15:14,908
Uh, yeah, I think
that's about right.

1409
01:15:14,977 --> 01:15:20,781
I was recently arrested
and charged for possession

1410
01:15:20,850 --> 01:15:23,518
of four ounces of cocaine.

1411
01:15:23,586 --> 01:15:25,786
And as it happened,
four ounces of cocaine

1412
01:15:25,855 --> 01:15:27,054
turned up missing

1413
01:15:27,123 --> 01:15:30,257
from the Sheriff's
Department Property Room.

1414
01:15:30,326 --> 01:15:32,493
You know what case
it was missing from?

1415
01:15:32,562 --> 01:15:34,528
I have no idea.

1416
01:15:34,597 --> 01:15:36,263
The Clark case. Your case.

1417
01:15:36,332 --> 01:15:38,532
As counsel of record,
you have access

1418
01:15:38,600 --> 01:15:39,900
to that cocaine, don't you?

1419
01:15:39,969 --> 01:15:42,369
Just what the hell
are you implying?

1420
01:15:42,438 --> 01:15:45,272
I'm not implying anything,
I'm saying right out loud.

1421
01:15:45,341 --> 01:15:47,775
You went in that room,

1422
01:15:47,844 --> 01:15:49,276
took four ounces of cocaine

1423
01:15:49,345 --> 01:15:51,779
out of the Clark evidence,
planted it in my car

1424
01:15:51,847 --> 01:15:54,548
in the hopes that my arrest
would end the investigation

1425
01:15:54,617 --> 01:15:56,216
into Ryan Bartell's murder.

1426
01:15:56,286 --> 01:15:57,318
This is outrageous!

1427
01:15:57,387 --> 01:15:59,298
Why would I do a
dumb thing like that?

1428
01:15:59,322 --> 01:16:01,555
It was dumb. I'm talking dumb!

1429
01:16:01,624 --> 01:16:04,958
Surely you had to know the
Clark case would come up,

1430
01:16:05,027 --> 01:16:06,571
and the cocaine
would turn up missing,

1431
01:16:06,595 --> 01:16:09,075
and there's not a chance in
hell you're going to get it back.

1432
01:16:09,132 --> 01:16:11,198
I didn't take it.

1433
01:16:11,267 --> 01:16:16,904
Mr. Lewis, the
cocaine found in my car

1434
01:16:16,973 --> 01:16:20,540
and the cocaine stored in
the Sheriff's Property Room

1435
01:16:20,609 --> 01:16:22,354
were analyzed. You
know what they found?

1436
01:16:22,378 --> 01:16:24,111
No, and I don't appreciate...

1437
01:16:24,180 --> 01:16:26,613
The two samples
were exactly the same.

1438
01:16:26,683 --> 01:16:29,483
86.2% cocaine.

1439
01:16:29,552 --> 01:16:32,653
13.8% mannite.

1440
01:16:32,722 --> 01:16:35,589
According to police chemists,

1441
01:16:35,658 --> 01:16:39,159
that would be an
extraordinary coincidence

1442
01:16:39,228 --> 01:16:41,006
unless both batches
were exactly the same.

1443
01:16:41,030 --> 01:16:42,129
What say, Commissioner?

1444
01:16:42,198 --> 01:16:43,842
Miss Lyle, isn't there something

1445
01:16:43,866 --> 01:16:45,644
you'd like to say at this point?

1446
01:16:45,668 --> 01:16:47,201
Yes, objection, Your Honor!

1447
01:16:47,270 --> 01:16:49,370
Um, there's no doubt

1448
01:16:49,438 --> 01:16:51,450
that his question is
leading to speculation,

1449
01:16:51,474 --> 01:16:53,941
and Mr. Matlock is engaging

1450
01:16:54,010 --> 01:16:55,688
in rampant speculation himself.

1451
01:16:55,712 --> 01:16:57,912
Sustained.

1452
01:17:02,919 --> 01:17:06,954
Uh, Mr. Lewis, how well
did you know Ryan Bartell?

1453
01:17:07,023 --> 01:17:10,324
I knew who he was.

1454
01:17:10,393 --> 01:17:11,725
How well do you know

1455
01:17:11,794 --> 01:17:15,829
Lee Kramer, David
Channing Ernie D'Amato?

1456
01:17:15,898 --> 01:17:18,632
I know who they are.

1457
01:17:18,701 --> 01:17:21,569
Are you aware that they
were recently arrested

1458
01:17:21,637 --> 01:17:25,939
in an alleged attempt to
transport huge sums of money

1459
01:17:26,008 --> 01:17:27,274
out of the country?

1460
01:17:27,343 --> 01:17:28,642
Yes.

1461
01:17:28,711 --> 01:17:33,013
Are you aware that an
attempt to catch them doing

1462
01:17:33,082 --> 01:17:36,416
the same thing at a different
location earlier in the day

1463
01:17:36,485 --> 01:17:37,784
was a dismal failure?

1464
01:17:37,854 --> 01:17:40,121
I've heard that.

1465
01:17:42,158 --> 01:17:44,958
Your office is next
to the sheriff's office,

1466
01:17:45,027 --> 01:17:47,761
and like you say, you
get about a good bit;

1467
01:17:47,830 --> 01:17:50,064
like to know what's going on.

1468
01:17:50,133 --> 01:17:52,232
I do like to keep up. Yeah.

1469
01:17:52,301 --> 01:17:55,469
You drop into the sheriff's
office almost every day.

1470
01:17:55,538 --> 01:17:57,571
We're old friends.

1471
01:17:57,640 --> 01:18:00,707
According to Deputy
McMasters, you dropped in

1472
01:18:00,776 --> 01:18:03,710
at the very moment
that he and the sheriff

1473
01:18:03,779 --> 01:18:05,690
were setting up
that first stakeout.

1474
01:18:05,714 --> 01:18:06,994
Well, I...

1475
01:18:07,050 --> 01:18:08,227
I could have done that, yeah.

1476
01:18:08,251 --> 01:18:09,917
Yeah, yeah. Now as you know,

1477
01:18:09,986 --> 01:18:12,586
the first stakeout failed.

1478
01:18:12,654 --> 01:18:17,290
When Deputy
McMasters, on his own,

1479
01:18:17,360 --> 01:18:18,520
set up the second stakeout,

1480
01:18:18,561 --> 01:18:20,861
and it was a roaring
success. Why?

1481
01:18:20,930 --> 01:18:22,630
I don't know.

1482
01:18:22,698 --> 01:18:26,000
It's because as soon as
you heard of the first stakeout,

1483
01:18:26,068 --> 01:18:27,334
you left the sheriff's office

1484
01:18:27,403 --> 01:18:29,803
and you called Lee
Kramer or Mr. Channing

1485
01:18:29,872 --> 01:18:31,504
or one of them and warned them.

1486
01:18:31,574 --> 01:18:32,706
No.

1487
01:18:32,775 --> 01:18:34,742
You've done little
favors for them for years,

1488
01:18:34,811 --> 01:18:36,088
haven't you? Objection.

1489
01:18:36,112 --> 01:18:37,112
Speculation.

1490
01:18:37,146 --> 01:18:38,912
No, Your Honor. No, Your Honor.

1491
01:18:38,981 --> 01:18:40,914
This is not speculation.

1492
01:18:40,983 --> 01:18:44,418
The proof is right
outside that door.

1493
01:18:44,487 --> 01:18:45,652
Overruled.

1494
01:18:45,721 --> 01:18:46,854
For the moment.

1495
01:18:46,923 --> 01:18:49,856
Thank you.

1496
01:18:49,926 --> 01:18:50,991
Uh, does the number

1497
01:18:51,060 --> 01:18:55,696
N-C-9-4-O-2-B-A
mean anything to you?

1498
01:18:55,765 --> 01:18:58,832
That's the registration
number on my boat.

1499
01:18:58,901 --> 01:19:02,503
Yeah. Silverly. Adamantium.

1500
01:19:02,571 --> 01:19:05,506
Sheriff Parks and
Deputy McMasters

1501
01:19:05,574 --> 01:19:08,942
found something
interesting in her hold.

1502
01:19:11,080 --> 01:19:13,347
Money.

1503
01:19:13,416 --> 01:19:18,652
More than a
million-and-a-half in cash.

1504
01:19:20,690 --> 01:19:22,656
Was that your reward

1505
01:19:22,725 --> 01:19:24,958
for planting the
cocaine in my car?

1506
01:19:25,027 --> 01:19:26,994
Or warning them?

1507
01:19:27,063 --> 01:19:29,763
Or maybe for
murdering Ryan Bartell?

1508
01:19:29,832 --> 01:19:31,765
Murder?

1509
01:19:32,835 --> 01:19:34,768
Now, wait a minute.

1510
01:19:34,837 --> 01:19:36,269
Just hold on.

1511
01:19:36,339 --> 01:19:38,905
I don't know where
that money came from.

1512
01:19:38,975 --> 01:19:40,919
Now, I might've done a
few favors for those guys,

1513
01:19:40,943 --> 01:19:43,088
like you said; I might've even
done a couple of other things

1514
01:19:43,112 --> 01:19:44,378
I shouldn't have done.

1515
01:19:44,447 --> 01:19:46,847
But I swear to you, I
never killed anybody.

1516
01:19:46,916 --> 01:19:49,895
I was at home that
morning. Ask my wife.

1517
01:19:49,919 --> 01:19:51,897
Ask my neighbor. I saw
him out in his backyard

1518
01:19:51,921 --> 01:19:53,853
and waved to him. Ask him.

1519
01:19:53,923 --> 01:19:56,856
Believe me, after
everything that's happened,

1520
01:19:56,926 --> 01:20:00,527
if I knew who murdered
Ryan Bartell, I'd sure tell you.

1521
01:20:00,595 --> 01:20:04,164
All I know is, it was not me.

1522
01:20:26,889 --> 01:20:29,256
Nothing further.

1523
01:20:33,295 --> 01:20:35,228
♪ ♪

1524
01:20:46,375 --> 01:20:48,309
You find anything? Not yet.

1525
01:20:51,380 --> 01:20:53,314
Hey, look here.

1526
01:20:53,382 --> 01:20:56,817
Ryan Bartell kept a new
knife in an old sheath.

1527
01:20:58,888 --> 01:21:00,487
That's not the
knife I saw in the bar

1528
01:21:00,556 --> 01:21:03,490
the night I broke up the fight
between Ryan and Spencer.

1529
01:21:04,994 --> 01:21:06,260
It's not?

1530
01:21:06,329 --> 01:21:08,262
Same type of knife,
but this one's brand-new.

1531
01:21:08,331 --> 01:21:09,930
Ryan's knife was old,

1532
01:21:09,999 --> 01:21:11,431
it was worn down
with sharpening,

1533
01:21:11,500 --> 01:21:13,433
and it was nicked.

1534
01:21:13,502 --> 01:21:15,102
Are you sure?

1535
01:21:15,171 --> 01:21:17,104
I'm positive.

1536
01:21:23,445 --> 01:21:25,412
<i>Now, let's see.</i>

1537
01:21:25,481 --> 01:21:28,014
You and Lee Kramer

1538
01:21:28,084 --> 01:21:32,086
and Ernie D'Amato
were running drugs

1539
01:21:32,154 --> 01:21:34,921
and laundering your
profits through here

1540
01:21:34,990 --> 01:21:36,356
to offshore banks, weren't you?

1541
01:21:37,460 --> 01:21:39,860
On the advice of my attorney,

1542
01:21:39,929 --> 01:21:41,839
I invoke my Fifth
Amendment right,

1543
01:21:41,863 --> 01:21:43,163
and refuse to answer

1544
01:21:43,232 --> 01:21:45,344
on the grounds that it may
intend to incriminate me.

1545
01:21:45,368 --> 01:21:47,300
Okay.

1546
01:21:47,370 --> 01:21:48,802
Who was Frank Hamilton?

1547
01:21:50,572 --> 01:21:52,839
He was a friend you
used to go hunting

1548
01:21:52,908 --> 01:21:54,375
and fishing with, wasn't he?

1549
01:21:54,443 --> 01:21:56,209
That would be...

1550
01:21:56,278 --> 01:21:58,345
That would be you and
Lee Kramer and Ernie

1551
01:21:58,414 --> 01:22:01,715
D'Amato and Ryan Bartell.

1552
01:22:01,784 --> 01:22:04,918
What happened to Frank Hamilton?

1553
01:22:04,987 --> 01:22:08,455
He was shot and killed in a
hunting accident, wasn't he?

1554
01:22:08,524 --> 01:22:11,225
Who fired the accidental shot

1555
01:22:11,294 --> 01:22:13,426
and killed him?

1556
01:22:15,798 --> 01:22:18,098
Who fired the shot?

1557
01:22:18,167 --> 01:22:20,367
Ryan Bartell.

1558
01:22:20,436 --> 01:22:22,369
Finally.

1559
01:22:22,438 --> 01:22:25,872
When did you first
meet Spencer Hamilton,

1560
01:22:25,942 --> 01:22:27,241
Frank's brother?

1561
01:22:27,310 --> 01:22:29,276
In the Roadhouse Bar.

1562
01:22:29,345 --> 01:22:31,356
Day before Ryan was murdered.

1563
01:22:31,380 --> 01:22:34,314
Hamilton told Ryan
he didn't believe

1564
01:22:34,383 --> 01:22:37,450
that the shooting
was accidental.

1565
01:22:37,520 --> 01:22:39,297
Accused him of
murdering his brother. Oh?

1566
01:22:39,321 --> 01:22:41,120
What'd Ryan do?

1567
01:22:41,190 --> 01:22:43,123
Ryan had a short fuse.

1568
01:22:43,192 --> 01:22:46,126
Hamilton called him a liar.

1569
01:22:46,195 --> 01:22:49,062
Ryan pulled a knife on him.

1570
01:22:49,131 --> 01:22:50,497
What kind of knife?

1571
01:22:50,566 --> 01:22:52,832
Hunting knife he always carried.

1572
01:22:52,901 --> 01:22:54,034
Aha.

1573
01:22:54,103 --> 01:22:56,903
Well, uh, what happened, uh,

1574
01:22:56,972 --> 01:22:59,939
after Deputy McMasters
came and broke it up?

1575
01:23:00,009 --> 01:23:02,942
Did Spencer leave?

1576
01:23:03,012 --> 01:23:04,277
Yeah.

1577
01:23:04,346 --> 01:23:06,113
Yeah. Stabbed
the knife in the table

1578
01:23:06,181 --> 01:23:07,481
and left.

1579
01:23:07,550 --> 01:23:10,483
Did you get the feeling

1580
01:23:10,553 --> 01:23:13,887
that he was gonna
stay around town

1581
01:23:13,956 --> 01:23:15,889
and start, uh, digging?

1582
01:23:19,829 --> 01:23:22,595
Surely you and your
friends must've known

1583
01:23:22,664 --> 01:23:24,597
that sooner or later,

1584
01:23:24,666 --> 01:23:27,200
we would stumble onto
your drug operation.

1585
01:23:27,269 --> 01:23:30,303
So you and your coconspirators

1586
01:23:30,373 --> 01:23:33,640
decided to kill Ryan Bartell

1587
01:23:33,709 --> 01:23:36,643
and frame Spencer Hamilton.

1588
01:23:36,712 --> 01:23:39,646
Killing two birds
with one stone,

1589
01:23:39,715 --> 01:23:41,326
so to speak. On the advice

1590
01:23:41,350 --> 01:23:42,782
of my attorney, I invoke my...

1591
01:23:42,852 --> 01:23:44,351
Now either you elected

1592
01:23:44,420 --> 01:23:46,352
on your own

1593
01:23:46,422 --> 01:23:50,156
or were chosen... to kill him.

1594
01:23:50,225 --> 01:23:54,828
But your reward was Ryan
Bartell's money, wasn't it?

1595
01:23:54,897 --> 01:23:57,164
On the advice of my attorney...

1596
01:23:57,233 --> 01:23:59,077
And then you got deeper
and deeper in trouble.

1597
01:23:59,101 --> 01:24:01,468
You planted that money
on Gordon Lewis's boat

1598
01:24:01,537 --> 01:24:03,504
to make him
look like the killer.

1599
01:24:03,572 --> 01:24:06,172
On the advice of my attorney...

1600
01:24:06,242 --> 01:24:08,508
All right, all right.

1601
01:24:10,613 --> 01:24:15,415
Do you recognize this knife?

1602
01:24:17,219 --> 01:24:20,087
That's Ryan's hunting knife.

1603
01:24:20,155 --> 01:24:21,454
Well, it is and it isn't.

1604
01:24:21,524 --> 01:24:24,391
It was found in
Mr. Bartell's home

1605
01:24:24,459 --> 01:24:28,695
and in the sheath that
Mr. Bartell always wore,

1606
01:24:28,764 --> 01:24:32,733
but Mr. Bartell
carried the same knife

1607
01:24:32,802 --> 01:24:34,401
five years or more.

1608
01:24:34,469 --> 01:24:37,104
It was all scratched and nicked.

1609
01:24:37,173 --> 01:24:38,939
This is brand-new, isn't it?

1610
01:24:39,008 --> 01:24:41,441
I don't know. Huh.

1611
01:24:41,510 --> 01:24:44,077
This knife was bought

1612
01:24:44,146 --> 01:24:46,847
on June the 10th at Hudson

1613
01:24:46,916 --> 01:24:48,715
Sporting Goods Store.

1614
01:24:48,784 --> 01:24:52,152
The clerk said the
man she sold it to,

1615
01:24:52,221 --> 01:24:55,388
showed her an old knife

1616
01:24:55,457 --> 01:24:59,092
and said he wanted
another one just like it.

1617
01:24:59,160 --> 01:25:00,760
That person was you, wasn't it?

1618
01:25:00,830 --> 01:25:03,297
No.

1619
01:25:03,365 --> 01:25:05,398
But why change knives?

1620
01:25:05,468 --> 01:25:07,668
There must've been
something on that old knife

1621
01:25:07,736 --> 01:25:09,535
that implicated you.

1622
01:25:09,605 --> 01:25:12,605
Maybe there was
some of your blood on it.

1623
01:25:12,675 --> 01:25:15,608
And that's why you
exchanged them, wasn't it?

1624
01:25:15,678 --> 01:25:18,178
I don't know what
you're talking about.

1625
01:25:21,350 --> 01:25:23,817
So, you say, "Why not

1626
01:25:23,886 --> 01:25:26,786
"just wash the blood
off the old knife?

1627
01:25:26,856 --> 01:25:29,323
Why get a new knife?"

1628
01:25:29,391 --> 01:25:32,525
And a few years ago,
that might've worked.

1629
01:25:32,594 --> 01:25:36,296
But anymore, police
science is so exact,

1630
01:25:36,365 --> 01:25:38,598
say you got a knife,
got blood on it...

1631
01:25:38,666 --> 01:25:41,968
You wash it off, can't be
seen with the naked eye...

1632
01:25:42,037 --> 01:25:44,704
The police with their
equipment can still find

1633
01:25:44,773 --> 01:25:47,006
what is called trace evidence.

1634
01:25:47,076 --> 01:25:50,911
Still traces of blood.

1635
01:25:50,980 --> 01:25:55,148
And they can be so exact,
they can tell you the type,

1636
01:25:55,217 --> 01:25:57,350
how long it's been there

1637
01:25:57,419 --> 01:26:00,754
and probably whose.

1638
01:26:04,026 --> 01:26:07,060
And you knew it
was yours, didn't you?

1639
01:26:07,129 --> 01:26:11,865
After you shot Ryan Bartell,
he stabbed you, didn't he?

1640
01:26:11,934 --> 01:26:13,099
Your Honor, I must object.

1641
01:26:13,168 --> 01:26:15,101
Pure speculation.

1642
01:26:15,171 --> 01:26:17,871
No, Your Honor, this
is not speculation at all.

1643
01:26:17,940 --> 01:26:19,940
We'll see,
Mr. Matlock. Overruled.

1644
01:26:20,009 --> 01:26:26,279
Let me try to be more
exact, Mr. Channing.

1645
01:26:26,348 --> 01:26:27,714
You shot him.

1646
01:26:27,782 --> 01:26:29,582
The first shot didn't kill him.

1647
01:26:29,651 --> 01:26:33,486
He rose up and plunged
his knife into your leg.

1648
01:26:33,555 --> 01:26:35,555
Your second shot did him in.

1649
01:26:35,624 --> 01:26:36,890
Uh...

1650
01:26:36,959 --> 01:26:39,359
You were real busy

1651
01:26:39,428 --> 01:26:41,894
the morning Ryan
Bartell was killed.

1652
01:26:41,964 --> 01:26:44,864
You shot him, he stabbed
you, you shot him again.

1653
01:26:44,933 --> 01:26:46,933
You took the
sheath off his body.

1654
01:26:47,002 --> 01:26:50,370
Picked up the knife, planted
the gun at Spencer's house.

1655
01:26:50,439 --> 01:26:53,540
Then you had to do something
about the blood on your leg.

1656
01:26:53,608 --> 01:26:55,442
Handkerchief or something.

1657
01:26:55,510 --> 01:26:57,811
Then you went to
Greenville, bought a new knife,

1658
01:26:57,880 --> 01:26:59,645
got rid of the old knife,

1659
01:26:59,714 --> 01:27:01,781
then you could
go to the hospital

1660
01:27:01,850 --> 01:27:04,083
and have something done

1661
01:27:04,153 --> 01:27:06,386
about the two-inch
gash in your leg.

1662
01:27:06,455 --> 01:27:07,687
How am I doing?

1663
01:27:07,756 --> 01:27:09,188
No.

1664
01:27:09,258 --> 01:27:11,658
That was your blood

1665
01:27:11,727 --> 01:27:12,992
on that old knife.

1666
01:27:13,061 --> 01:27:16,129
We called all the
hospitals in the vicinity,

1667
01:27:16,198 --> 01:27:19,600
and there's several people
at the hospital up in Greenville

1668
01:27:19,668 --> 01:27:20,668
who remember you.

1669
01:27:20,703 --> 01:27:23,469
Said you came in
with a bloody thigh.

1670
01:27:23,538 --> 01:27:25,638
It wasn't me.

1671
01:27:25,708 --> 01:27:27,152
There are two people
in the gallery right now

1672
01:27:27,176 --> 01:27:28,875
ready to identify you.

1673
01:27:28,944 --> 01:27:31,344
Well, they're wrong.

1674
01:27:31,413 --> 01:27:35,748
There's a simple
way we can settle this.

1675
01:27:35,817 --> 01:27:40,120
The scar, if there is
one, would be about here.

1676
01:27:40,189 --> 01:27:42,889
We can go in judge's
chambers, take a look,

1677
01:27:42,957 --> 01:27:46,326
or I've got a pocket knife.

1678
01:27:46,395 --> 01:27:50,664
We can split your
pants... up about that far.

1679
01:27:50,733 --> 01:27:53,800
There's no scar,
you're home free.

1680
01:27:53,869 --> 01:27:55,546
We'll get you a
new pair of pants.

1681
01:27:55,570 --> 01:27:56,903
What say?

1682
01:28:13,788 --> 01:28:18,158
I have got a little scar
from when I was working

1683
01:28:18,226 --> 01:28:20,526
on a propeller of my boat.

1684
01:28:34,143 --> 01:28:37,076
Nothing further.

1685
01:28:45,721 --> 01:28:47,932
We find the defendant,
Spencer Hamilton,

1686
01:28:47,956 --> 01:28:49,236
not guilty.

1687
01:28:49,291 --> 01:28:51,091
Thank you, ladies and gentlemen.

1688
01:28:52,161 --> 01:28:54,461
This court is adjourned.

1689
01:29:02,604 --> 01:29:04,104
Okay, see, now,
this is important.

1690
01:29:04,172 --> 01:29:05,638
Don't touch this;
this is the flash.

1691
01:29:05,707 --> 01:29:07,318
Just look through this
little square box here

1692
01:29:07,342 --> 01:29:09,019
and then push that... I
know how to take a picture!

1693
01:29:09,043 --> 01:29:12,078
Thank you. What a
nice-looking group here.

1694
01:29:12,147 --> 01:29:13,246
Thanks, thanks.

1695
01:29:13,315 --> 01:29:14,525
Well, Mr. Matlock,
thanks for everything.

1696
01:29:14,549 --> 01:29:15,960
And good luck, good luck to you.

1697
01:29:15,984 --> 01:29:17,904
Thanks, Conrad. Yeah, thank you.

1698
01:29:17,952 --> 01:29:19,352
Me first!

1699
01:29:19,421 --> 01:29:21,454
Della Basnight.

1700
01:29:21,523 --> 01:29:23,667
Uh, Conrad, I'm glad to have
met you and worked with you.

1701
01:29:23,691 --> 01:29:25,492
And so am I glad to
have met you, Mr. Matlock.

1702
01:29:25,560 --> 01:29:27,560
This is Jimmy;
Jimmy is a Basnight.

1703
01:29:27,629 --> 01:29:29,039
It's good, huh? Yeah.

1704
01:29:29,063 --> 01:29:30,575
Especially winning. Aw.

1705
01:29:30,599 --> 01:29:32,376
Bobby... Winning tastes
good, doesn't it? Yes.

1706
01:29:32,400 --> 01:29:34,640
Bobby's an Owen, but
he married a Basnight.

1707
01:29:34,669 --> 01:29:36,914
Look, uh, next time you
come, we'll keep it a secret.

1708
01:29:36,938 --> 01:29:38,682
I'll take you blue fishing. Oh!

1709
01:29:38,706 --> 01:29:40,973
This is Betty Alice Mann.

1710
01:29:41,042 --> 01:29:44,477
Bluefish, listen, if you
come to Atlanta, I know...

1711
01:29:44,546 --> 01:29:46,457
This is John
Wilton; he's a cousin.

1712
01:29:46,481 --> 01:29:49,081
But hell, everybody
on the island's a cousin!

1713
01:29:49,151 --> 01:29:52,218
You come to Atlanta, I know
a place where there's catfish.

1714
01:29:52,287 --> 01:29:54,920
You can't catch any, but
you can buy all you want!

1715
01:29:54,989 --> 01:29:56,556
This is Mama!

1716
01:29:56,625 --> 01:29:58,358
Smile.

1717
01:29:58,426 --> 01:30:00,466
Aah...

1718
01:30:02,497 --> 01:30:06,999
♪ When it's peach
picking time in Georgia ♪

1719
01:30:07,068 --> 01:30:10,704
♪ Apple picking
time in Tennessee ♪

1720
01:30:10,772 --> 01:30:14,574
♪ Cotton picking
time in Mississippi ♪

1721
01:30:14,643 --> 01:30:19,379
♪ Everybody picks on me. ♪

1722
01:30:19,448 --> 01:30:22,582
Playing our song.

1723
01:30:24,118 --> 01:30:26,319
Well, Conrad!

1724
01:30:26,388 --> 01:30:27,687
Get in here!

1725
01:30:27,756 --> 01:30:29,266
What you doing in Atlanta?!

1726
01:30:29,290 --> 01:30:30,790
Oh, I had a little vacation.

1727
01:30:30,859 --> 01:30:32,625
How much vacation? A lot.

1728
01:30:32,694 --> 01:30:33,926
I never really took one.

1729
01:30:33,995 --> 01:30:35,428
You never... sit down!

1730
01:30:36,898 --> 01:30:39,599
You know, in Manteo, it's
hard to tell vacation from work.

1731
01:30:39,668 --> 01:30:41,946
Neither one's exactly
strenuous. Oh, yeah.

1732
01:30:41,970 --> 01:30:43,080
How is Manteo?

1733
01:30:43,104 --> 01:30:44,682
Oh, Manteo...
Manteo's great. Yeah?

1734
01:30:44,706 --> 01:30:47,174
Everybody misses you,
wishes you'd come back.

1735
01:30:47,242 --> 01:30:48,775
Oh, I will, I will.

1736
01:30:48,844 --> 01:30:51,377
So, what are your
plans during vacation?

1737
01:30:51,447 --> 01:30:53,447
Well, I-I... I have
no real plans.

1738
01:30:53,515 --> 01:30:56,349
I thought I'd just
stop and see you.

1739
01:30:58,120 --> 01:31:00,486
So...

1740
01:31:00,556 --> 01:31:02,455
So, I have, so I
guess I'll be going.

1741
01:31:02,524 --> 01:31:03,835
What?

1742
01:31:03,859 --> 01:31:05,139
Well, wait.

1743
01:31:05,193 --> 01:31:06,473
Wait a minute.

1744
01:31:06,528 --> 01:31:10,330
Ar-Are you saying
you've got some

1745
01:31:10,399 --> 01:31:12,932
free time while
you're on vacation?

1746
01:31:13,001 --> 01:31:14,300
Yeah, a little.

1747
01:31:14,369 --> 01:31:15,847
Why? Oh.

1748
01:31:19,374 --> 01:31:22,208
Well, see, I've got this client,

1749
01:31:22,277 --> 01:31:25,245
and he's up for murder
one, but he didn't do it.

1750
01:31:25,313 --> 01:31:28,414
I'm pretty sure
it was a mob hit.

1751
01:31:28,483 --> 01:31:30,662
But now I've got to prove it.

1752
01:31:30,686 --> 01:31:32,430
Mob, huh? Yeah. It's dangerous.

1753
01:31:32,454 --> 01:31:34,154
I mean...

1754
01:31:34,222 --> 01:31:35,521
there's some...

1755
01:31:35,590 --> 01:31:37,802
very bad dudes.

1756
01:31:37,826 --> 01:31:40,627
Gonna take a lot
of investigating.

1757
01:31:40,696 --> 01:31:44,164
A lot of investigating?
A lot, yeah.

1758
01:31:47,436 --> 01:31:49,869
You know, if you
need some help...

1759
01:31:49,938 --> 01:31:51,738
Oh, no, no, no, no, no.

1760
01:31:51,807 --> 01:31:53,551
I wouldn't want to
interfere with your holiday...

1761
01:31:53,575 --> 01:31:56,576
You wouldn't, no, you wouldn't.
No, I-I-I really want to help.

1762
01:31:56,644 --> 01:31:58,244
Well...

1763
01:32:00,315 --> 01:32:02,882
And it shows, doesn't it?

1764
01:32:04,652 --> 01:32:05,985
Yeah, it does, Conrad.

1765
01:32:06,054 --> 01:32:08,399
It's, it's, it's all
over you. Yeah.

1766
01:32:08,423 --> 01:32:10,334
You like a little
excitement, huh?

1767
01:32:10,358 --> 01:32:11,825
Yeah, well, you need some help.

1768
01:32:11,894 --> 01:32:13,838
Looks like we were
meant for each other.

1769
01:32:13,862 --> 01:32:14,995
I hope so.

1770
01:32:15,063 --> 01:32:15,996
B and C. No.

1771
01:32:16,064 --> 01:32:18,464
C and B. C and B.

9999
00:00:0,500 --> 00:00:2,00
